  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why does a car engine get so hot?",
      "a": "Burning fuel releases a lot of heat, and only about a quarter to a third of it becomes movement. The rest heats the engine and exhaust, so it must be carried away.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do engines need cooling?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the water pump do?",
      "a": "The water pump is a spinning impeller, driven by the engine or an electric motor, that pushes coolant round the engine and radiator.",
      "asks": [
        "What moves the coolant?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a thermostat do in a car?",
      "a": "The thermostat is a valve that stays shut while the engine is cold and opens when the coolant is hot, at about 90 degrees Celsius. This warms the engine quickly, then keeps it at a steady temperature.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a car engine need a thermostat?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a radiator cool the engine?",
      "a": "Hot coolant flows through many thin tubes covered in fins. Air passing between the fins takes the heat away, so the liquid comes out cooler.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a car radiator for?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a car have a cooling fan?",
      "a": "When the car is slow or stopped in traffic, not enough air rushes through the radiator. An electric fan switches on to pull air through, and may keep running for a while after you stop.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the fan run after I stop the car?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Coolant is usually water mixed with antifreeze, which lowers its freezing point and raises its boiling point.",
    "The cooling system is kept under pressure, which raises the boiling point of the coolant above 100 degrees Celsius.",
    "A car's heater warms the cabin using hot coolant from the engine."
  ],
